# Optional companion to Debian.sudoers, for hosts running the classic sudo from
# sudo.ws. Install it as a second drop-in whose name carries no dot, for example
# /etc/sudoers.d/linuxfabrik-monitoring-plugins-logging: sudo skips a file in
# /etc/sudoers.d whose name contains a "." or ends in "~".
#
# The settings below live apart from the rules rather than next to them because
# sudo-rs, the default sudo on Ubuntu 26.04, does not know them and prints a
# warning for each on every single sudo call by any user, including the ones that
# have nothing to do with monitoring. Verified against sudo-rs 0.2.13, the version
# Ubuntu 26.04 ships. See
# https://github.com/Linuxfabrik/monitoring-plugins/issues/1493
#
# Splitting them off is safe because sudo builds a single policy out of every file
# below /etc/sudoers.d and looks an alias up only once a command is matched,
# against that finished tree (`parsed_policy` and `alias_get()` in
# plugins/sudoers/alias.c). A `Defaults!LF_NAGIOS` here therefore finds the alias
# defined in the other file whichever of the two sudo reads first, measured with
# the companion sorted both before and after the main file on Rocky 8, 9 and 10,
# Debian 11, 12 and 13 and Ubuntu 22.04 and 24.04.
#
# `requiretty` is deliberately not turned off any more: sudoers(5) documents the
# flag as "off by default", none of the distributions above ships it in
# /etc/sudoers, and sudo ran without a tty on every one of them.

# A plugin runs unattended, so there is no session to set up for it, and no login
# record that would have to be written and torn down for every check.
Defaults:nagios !pam_session

# A monitored host runs dozens of checks every minute, each of them through sudo.
# Logging each one buries whatever else reaches the authentication log: without
# the two lines below a single check costs five entries, the sudo command line
# plus the PAM session being opened and closed.
#
# `!log_allowed` rather than `!syslog`: it silences the calls the policy permits
# and leaves `log_denied` alone, so anything this rule set refuses is still
# recorded. It also works on both implementations - sudo has it since 1.8.29 and
# sudo-rs since 0.2.15 - which is what will allow these lines to move back into
# the main file once no supported release ships anything older. Ubuntu 26.04 is
# on sudo-rs 0.2.13 today, which is why they still sit here.
#
# Drop these two lines to keep the audit trail for the plugin calls as well.
Defaults!LF_NAGIOS !log_allowed
Defaults!LF_LIBRENMS_VALIDATE !log_allowed
